WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.720
The second episode of "Halo" is finally available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:06.400
and fans are already scouring each frame&nbsp;
to find every hidden game reference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.320
Did you spot them all? Here are the Easter&nbsp;
eggs you missed in "Halo" episode 2.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.560
The second episode of "Halo" starts with&nbsp;
a flashback to Master Chief's youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.480
As a Spartan recruit, the young John is&nbsp;
shown confronting one of his close friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.680
a trainee named Soren, who's&nbsp;
trying to escape Reach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.120
It's revealed that John originally planned to&nbsp;
leave with him, but doesn't go through with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.760
the desertion in the end. It's at that point&nbsp;
that John shows mercy to his fleeing friend.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.640
"You've got five minutes, and then&nbsp;
I'm waking up the entire camp."

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.880
He may not appear in the video games, but Soren&nbsp;
does appear in the short story collection "Halo:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.880
Evolutions." Specifically, he's the main&nbsp;
character of a story titled "Pariah”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.760
which follows him from a brutal outer colony&nbsp;
upbringing, being experimented on by Dr. Halsey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.880
Soren's body reacts negatively&nbsp;
to the Spartan augmentations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.880
leaving his body deformed. And like in&nbsp;
the show, he eventually chooses to defect.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.800
"Halo: Evolutions" never confirms&nbsp;
what happens to Soren after being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.040
shot down by the UNSC during his escape&nbsp;
attempt. The show, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.120
reveals that he actually managed to get away and&nbsp;
build a life for himself away from prying eyes.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.840
Many of the other returning characters in&nbsp;
"Halo" make their game connections obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.880
Players will quickly recall Dr. Catherine Halsey,&nbsp;
Captain Jacob Keyes, and of course Master Chief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.680
himself. However, there are a couple of characters&nbsp;
in the UNSC that only fans will recognize.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.040
One of those characters is Admiral Margaret&nbsp;
Parangosky, Dr. Halsey's direct superior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.760
Parangosky has her origins in the "Halo" books,&nbsp;
but she appears in a few of the games as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.880
Her role in the TV series is as the director&nbsp;
of the UNSC's Office of Naval Intelligence.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.120
Easier to miss is Lord Hood, one of the&nbsp;
highest-ranking officers of the UNSC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.800
who first appears in a conversation&nbsp;
with Dr. Halsey. While he isn't named,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.480
shots during the council meeting show that&nbsp;
he wears a nametag with "Hood" written on it.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.400
Beyond just characters, there are&nbsp;
several places fans will also recognize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.360
After going rogue, Master Chief takes Kwan Ha to&nbsp;
the place he believes she'll be safe: the Rubble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.080
A renegade human colony built in an amalgam of&nbsp;
asteroids, the Rubble appears most prominently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.160
in the novel "Halo: The Cole Protocol." In&nbsp;
the book, many of the Rubble's inhabitants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.720
are refugees from Madrigal, which seems to be&nbsp;
referenced in the show by Kwan's arrival there.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.360
However, there are a lot of differences between&nbsp;
the Rubble of the old "Halo" canon and the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.960
For starters, in "The Cole Protocol," the&nbsp;
Rubble isn't just inhabited by humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.440
but also by the Kig-Yar, one of the member&nbsp;
species of the Covenant. Secondly, the colony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.720
in the book is largely operated by an AI called&nbsp;
Juliana. Lastly and perhaps most significantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.280
the Rubble is ultimately destroyed by the Covenant&nbsp;
in the books, forcing its residents to flee.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.720
The Kig-Yar plotline and the eventual&nbsp;
destruction of the Rubble could eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.840
take place in the "Halo" series as&nbsp;
well, but that remains to be seen.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.720
After reuniting with Soren on the Rubble,&nbsp;
Master Chief is introduced to a man named Reth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.400
a human who was driven mad after being&nbsp;
imprisoned by the Covenant. It's implied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.960
that Reth was experimented on and possibly even&nbsp;
tortured, all as part of the Covenant's mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.520
to discover and utilize Forerunner artifacts.&nbsp;
Reth even makes an important revelation.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.360
"What is that ring?"
"A door."

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.800
Though he doesn't call it a Halo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.240
he clearly understands that it's&nbsp;
a weapon of genocidal proportions.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.400
Interestingly, Reth is actually a reference&nbsp;
to the novel "Halo: The Cole Protocol."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.320
In the book, Reth is the name of one of the&nbsp;
leaders of the Kig-Yar who engage in trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.800
operations with the humans of the Rubble. He's a&nbsp;
treacherous and scheming Covenant commander who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.280
ultimately causes problems for both factions, and&nbsp;
it's certainly an interesting choice for the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.920
to name a human after him. Reth could simply be an&nbsp;
Easter egg for fans who've read the "Halo" books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.280
but his name could also foreshadow a&nbsp;
darker role in the episodes to come.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.160
Finally, at the end of the episode, when Master&nbsp;
Chief turns himself in to the UNSC, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.880
brief glimpse and mention of a frigate called the&nbsp;
Stalwart Dawn. In the video game "Halo: Reach,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.960
the Stalwart Dawn takes part in the defense of&nbsp;
the eponymous planet from a surprise attack by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.440
the Covenant. Things don't end well for the humans&nbsp;
on Reach, and the inclusion of the Stalwart Dawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.600
could be a hint at things to come. The Fall of&nbsp;
Reach is one of the most important events in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.160
the entire "Halo" timeline, so it seems inevitable&nbsp;
that it will be brought into the show eventually.

